{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey security deposit law (transfer, return and penalties)","requirement":"On sale or transfer, the new owner must obtain the tenant's deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, paying interest and returning it. Willfully withholding a deposit of a tenant receiving government assistance draws a $500 to $2,000 civil penalty.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"All residential landlord-tenant relationships; the civil penalty applies where the tenant has received State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceeding; unlawful use of deposit monies may bring a disorderly persons charge with a fine of at least $200, up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings, which are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after construction is completed.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rents that are subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law ( N.J.S.A. 2A:42 -84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Exempts covered buildings from municipal rent control ordinances.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act notice procedure for rent increases","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; the landlord must terminate the old lease and offer a new lease at the increased rent with written notice before evicting for nonpayment of an increase, and the increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; any rent increase must comply with municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"Increase must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion cost rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not they qualify for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A residential landlord may evict or refuse to renew a lease only for good cause proved in court; a Notice to Quit must be served for all good cause evictions except nonpayment of rent, and a Notice to Cease may also be needed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; the Anti-Eviction Act (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1 to 61.12) protects blameless tenants.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source of lawful income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in the rental of housing based on source of lawful income, along with race, sex, disability and the other characteristics the Law Against Discrimination lists.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex or age-restricted housing as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Exempt: two-family owner-occupied dwellings and rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes."},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex (for example YMCA) and age-restricted housing as to familial status.","penalty":"Under the LAD, violators are subject to substantial fines and penalties, up to $10,000 for a first offense; a discriminating landlord may also have to pay damages and rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
